<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en" class="dark"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"/><title>Science | Boulder Inversion Tracker</title><script src="https://cdn.tailwindcss.com"></script><style>body{background:#060911;color:#e2e8f0;font-family:system-ui,sans-serif}a{color:#67e8f9}.card{background:#0f172a;border:1px solid #334155;border-radius:20px;padding:28px}</style></head><body class="min-h-screen"><header class="border-b border-slate-700 p-5"><nav class="max-w-4xl mx-auto flex justify-between gap-4"><strong>Boulder Inversion Tracker — Science</strong><div class="space-x-5"><a href="/">Dashboard</a><a href="/guide.html">Guide</a></div></nav></header><main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto p-6 space-y-6">
<section class="card space-y-3"><h1 class="text-2xl font-extrabold">Three different physical events</h1><p><strong>Temperature inversion:</strong> locally warmer air above colder air; a vertically resolved thermal phenomenon. <strong>Airport fog:</strong> measured near-surface visibility and clouds at a weather station. <strong>Scenic cloud sea:</strong> low clouds in the Boulder area with clear viewing at a particular higher elevation. None is equivalent to the others.</p><p>The current airport-fog ML output is trained with independent METAR observations. It does not establish a measured inversion or above-cloud summit event.</p></section>
<section class="card space-y-3"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Numerical weather forecasts become ML features</h2><p>Open-Meteo serves individual historical and live NOAA GFS runs. We ingest surface forecast humidity, visibility, weather codes, clouds and wind, plus atmospheric temperature, humidity, wind, cloud cover and geopotential height at pressure levels. XGBoost also receives local time and forecast lead (hours between run initialization and valid time). The CNN receives five interpolated vertical channels: temperature, humidity, east-west and north-south wind, and forecast cloud cover.</p><p>All training and inference weather features come from a particular initialized GFS forecast run. The strict lead-time bound is less than 72 hours. An issued forecast is not replaced with the hindsight-laden stitched historical forecast archive.</p></section>
<section class="card space-y-3"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Independent observations and labels</h2><p>Airport KBDU/METAR visibility, `FG` weather reports and reported ceiling layers define the station fog target. Reports are retrieved independently from the Iowa Environmental Mesonet. Missing/ambiguous observations are not treated as clear conditions. A separate model may learn the altitude of the <strong>base</strong> of reported low BKN/OVC cloud at the airport, using its 5,288 ft MSL field elevation. Cloud <strong>top</strong> cannot be recovered from airport METAR data.</p><p>If independently verified mountain visibility observations are supplied with a timestamp and elevation, a third, elevation-conditioned XGBoost model can learn the joint airport-fog-and-summit-clear outcome. With no such data, the website displays “Not trained” for that outcome.</p></section>
<section class="card space-y-3"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold">Training and uncertainty</h2><p>Train/validation/test partitions are chronological by forecast valid time, with a 72-hour gap to reduce leakage from overlapping forecast runs. Both fog classifiers can be calibrated on validation events. Holdout Brier, log-loss, ROC AUC and average precision are reported; rare-event class balance and validation selection remain concerns.</p><p>A model with positive GFS temperature gradients is only displaying a <em>forecast inversion signal</em>; there is no independent observed inversion training target yet. Keep the distinction visible in scientific interpretation.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-3">Primary sources</h2><p><a href="https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/single-runs-api">Open-Meteo Single Runs</a> · <a href="https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/gfs-api">GFS variable documentation</a> · <a href="https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/request/download.phtml?network=CO_ASOS">IEM observed METAR</a>. Full reproducibility instructions and caveats are in <a href="https://github.com/samadler8/invert-boulder">the repository README</a>.</p></section></main></body></html>
