<file path="README.md">
# Boulder Inversion Tracker — short-range observation-trained edition

A local website and two small machine-learning models for **0–<72-hour** forecasts around Boulder, Colorado. Written for a MacBook; trained weights are small enough to commit to GitHub after you train them. **No pretrained model files are included** because independent historical observations and live public API requests have not been downloaded or validated here.

## What it actually predicts

| Outcome | Ground truth | Trained model | What is shown |
|---|---|---|---|
| Airport fog/low ceiling | Independent KBDU airport METAR, conservative binary thresholds | XGBoost + 1D CNN | Calibrated model estimates of that **operational label**, NOT inversion probabilities |
| Low cloud **base** elevation | Independent METAR `BKN`/`OVC`/`VV` ceiling heights, ft above airport terrain + 5,288 ft MSL | Separate XGBoost regression, **only if enough labels** | Predicted *base* elevation in feet MSL and validation residual band |
| Scenic cloud sea above individual landmark | Human-verified `observed_summit_clear` at that elevation, joined with independently observed Boulder-area fog | Optional elevation-conditioned XGBoost classifier | Per-landmark scenic-event estimate **only for elevations with training labels** |
| Atmospheric inversion | No independent vertical soundings included | **Not trained** | GFS-predicted vertical temperature warming diagnostic, NOT probability |
| Actual cloud top | No reliable cloud-top observations included | **Not trained** | *Unknown*; cloud base must never be mistaken for cloud top |

The airport is northeast of downtown Boulder; airport fog != city-center fog. Actual visibility and summit cloud decks may differ significantly across the foothills. A low airport METAR cloud base **cannot** tell us whether a summit is above a cloud layer.

### Why single GFS runs rather than stitched historical forecasts?

Training features use historical **GFS initialization times** from Open-Meteo's **Single Runs API**, released for GFS and most other models from **April 2, 2026**. The valid timestamps are separated from forecast issue timestamps; lead time is an explicit XGBoost feature. Each run includes only forecast lead `0 <= lead < 72` hours (less than three days). This avoids treating hindsight from a stitched near-real-time forecast as though it were an issued 3-day forecast.

Historical valid times can appear in several overlapping issued runs. We retain their distinct lead times, and the train/validation/test split is by **valid time** with a 72-hour gap so different predictions verifying the same observation cannot straddle a split. Future improvements should also group synoptic weather events, include lead-stratified evaluation, and reserve a separate calibration set.

The live server retrieves an actual **initialized GFS run** (after an assumed 7-hour publication lag, with fallbacks to older 6-hour cycles) and discards hours before the current time or with lead >=72. It may show ~54–65 future hours, rather than a full 72 future hours. It does not silently substitute stitched forecasts if an initialized run is missing.

This first implementation uses **GFS only** for consistent archive/live input schemas. HRRR may be more useful in the foothills at shorter lead times, but mixing HRRR into a GFS-trained model without a separately aligned archived training pipeline would cause train/serve drift. HRRR's forecast horizon and vertical cloud-cover availability also differ.

## Setup (macOS)

Requires Python 3.11+ and internet access for download or live inference. From the repository root:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
```

### 1. Download independent airport observations

```bash
python -m inversion.observations \
  --start 2026-04-02 --end 2026-10-20 \
  --output data/observed_bdu.csv
```

This downloads raw METAR reports from the [Iowa Environmental Mesonet](https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/request/download.phtml?network=CO_ASOS), selecting station `BDU` in `CO_ASOS`. It keeps observations nearest each UTC hour, independently of the weather model. It stores `observed_fog` as:

- **1**: reported visibility <=1 statute mile *and* fog weather code (`FG`/`FZFG`) or reported low ceiling <=1,000 ft AGL;
- **0**: visibility >=5 miles and no fog weather code;
- **blank**: ambiguous or missing (NOT a negative).

The METAR observed low-cloud base is the lowest `BKN`, `OVC`, or `VV` reported layer at <=5,000 ft AGL converted to ft MSL using the airport elevation. That only describes **cloud base**, not cloud top, and may be missing.

**This is a deliberately strict operational proxy for airport fog, not a universal meteorological inversion definition.** Many samples will be unlabeled, and there might not be enough positive cases in the archive. In that case training correctly refuses to produce a model. Inspect `data/observed_bdu.csv` and station sensor continuity before proceeding.

### 2. Train both models on archived issued forecasts

For example, train through October 20, 2026 (leave at least 3 days for verification observations):

```bash
python -m inversion.train \
  --start 2026-04-02 --end 2026-10-20 \
  --observations data/observed_bdu.csv \
  --run-every-hours 24
```

`--run-every-hours 24` downloads one 00 UTC run per day; 12 or 6 downloads more forecasts per day, increasing time and disk/network use. Raw run JSON is cached in `data/gfs_runs/` (Git-ignored). HTTP failures stop training instead of fabricating weather input. Some variables or model runs may be unavailable; support depends on provider coverage. To retry, rerun after the outage; completed runs remain cached.

**Requirements for successful training:** At least 150 labeled training examples overall, positive and negative observations in each chronological subset, a meaningful split after 72-hour purges, and complete vertical GFS profiles. If fog is too rare in the available Apr–Oct 2026 archive, the script raises an informative error. Do not try to fix that with synthetic training labels and claim real-world probabilities.

Trained artifacts (automatically generated):

```
models/
  xgb.json           airport-fog gradient-boosted trees
  cnn.pt             vertical-profile CNN + train-only normalizer
  manifest.json      feature schema, provenance, calibration, test scores
  cloud_base.json    optional airport low-cloud-base regressor
  scenic.json        optional labeled elevation-conditioned scenic classifier
```

The `.gitignore` excludes downloaded raw data but **does not exclude model weights**. Commit trained models only after auditing their data provenance, holdout metrics, and licensing.

### 3. Optional independently labeled summit events

METAR airport reports do **not** tell us whether Green Mountain or the First Flatiron is above the clouds. If you collect independent, timestamped summit camera/hiker observations, save them as a CSV:

```csv
 timestamp,target_elevation_ft,observed_summit_clear
 2026-08-10T12:00:00Z,8100,1
 2026-08-10T13:00:00Z,8100,0
```

The timestamps must be UTC. `observed_summit_clear=1` means the landmark itself is independently observed clear, not necessarily a scenic cloud sea. The scenic **joint** target is `observed_airport_fog AND observed_summit_clear`. We omit the unknown airport-fog hours, rather than treating them as negatives. The altitude is an **explicit model input**, and the optional classifier is trained **only if** its observed labels support a proper chronological split. Only elevations appearing in the training labels are shown as having modeled scenic probabilities (never extrapolate to unverified altitudes automatically).

Train with both sources:

```bash
python -m inversion.train \
  --start 2026-04-02 --end 2026-10-20 \
  --observations data/observed_bdu.csv \
  --summit-labels data/summit_observations.csv
```

If summit labels are absent or insufficient, the website says **Not trained**, rather than guessing scenic success. Adding cloud-base observations is **not** a replacement for summit labels.

### 4. Start the local website

```bash
python -m uvicorn inversion.server:app --reload
```

Open **http://127.0.0.1:8000**. Other users can download the GitHub repository, install requirements, and use your committed artifacts without retraining. They still need internet to fetch new GFS forecasts (as well as Tailwind/FontAwesome/Google Fonts if not bundled locally).

HTTP endpoints: `/api/health` and `/api/forecast` (`?refresh=true` bypasses the 30-minute forecast response cache). `/api/forecast` includes forecast initialization time, lead time, both fog estimates, optional reported-cloud-base prediction, optional supervised scenic predictions and explicit `null` cloud top. It returns errors when models or live forecast data are unavailable.

## Tests

```bash
python -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
python -m pytest -q
node --check dashboard.js
```

Tests use **only synthetic fixtures** to check file formats, run lead/time handling, model shapes, conservation of missing labels, model save/load/reload, optional elevation models, and the offline local API. Passing these tests does **NOT** measure scientific forecast skill. The live Open-Meteo/IEM endpoints could not be invoked from the build environment; run these commands on a networked machine and inspect actual responses.

## Architecture

```
inversion/config.py         Shared schema, elevations, pressure levels, model/version
inversion/weather.py        Open-Meteo GFS Single Runs HTTP download + cache
inversion/observations.py   Independent Iowa Mesonet/METAR observation downloader
inversion/features.py       Vertical interpolation; XGBoost and CNN input schema
inversion/models.py         XGBoost + small CNN, sigmoid, Platt calibration, metrics
inversion/train.py          Match issued runs to METAR; chronological split; optional base/scenic targets
inversion/predict.py        Load exported weights, create model outputs per valid hour/elevation
inversion/server.py         Local FastAPI and 30-min response caching
dashboard.js                Interactive short-range dashboard, model caveats, scenarios
index.html / guide.html / science.html   Site and public documentation
tests/test_pipeline.py      Offline smoke + integration tests
```

## Scientific and engineering caveats

1. **Not a verified thermal-inversion classifier.** Airport METAR measures fog/visibility/cloud bases; vertical thermal inversions require independent radiosonde or similar vertical observations. The forecast `forecast_inversion_warming_signal` is simply a GFS temperature-profile diagnostic.
2. **Not a cloud-top estimator.** The optional regressor predicts a cloud **base** conditional on low-cloud ceiling reports. Its 80% validation residual band is not a conditional conformal prediction interval. It cannot identify the cloud top or a safe hike above clouds.
3. **Rare events.** Boulder Airport fog events may be too scarce for reliable model fitting or calibration; the short GFS run archive (from April 2026) compounds that limitation. More features cannot repair missing labels.
4. **Scenic-event dataset.** Hand/automated camera labels need quality control, daylight handling, repeat observations and enough cases across elevations and seasons. No automated webcam scraper is bundled because reliable permission/access and camera placement have not been established.
5. **Meteorological resolution.** GFS is too coarse for some Front Range topographic effects. HRRR needs its own archive/feature validation before inclusion. Pressure-profile interpolation provides consistent grids, not finer actual measurements.
6. **Lead-time and deployment.** Forecast run availability can lag initialization (often hours). The site always enforces `<72 hours since issue`, and new weather still requires an internet connection. Weather model updates over time can cause domain shift.
7. **Evaluations.** Probabilities are calibrated on validation observations. The 72h split purge avoids trivial cross-run leakage but repeated weather-event dependence remains. Test-set Brier/ROC/AP are conditional on the sample selection, not guaranteed generalization. Next measure separate 0–24h, 24–48h and 48–72h performance against raw GFS weather-code/visibility baselines.
8. **API terms.** Review [Open-Meteo terms](https://open-meteo.com/en/terms) and [IEM terms](https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/), and consider attribution, frequency limits and licensing before distributing commercially.

## Source documentation

- [Open-Meteo Single Runs API](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/single-runs-api) — UTC `run` initialization and run archive dates.
- [Open-Meteo GFS & HRRR](https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/gfs-api) — forecast variables and GFS variant availability.
- [Iowa Environmental Mesonet ASOS request](https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/request/download.phtml?network=CO_ASOS) — direct METAR observations, `vsby`, `skyc*`, `skyl*`.
- [Boulder Municipal Airport elevation](https://www.airnav.com/airport/KBDU) — estimated airport field elevation 5,288 ft MSL.
